WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.794
A counter-deception response begins with preservation and containment of harm.

00:00:03.794 --> 00:00:07.488
Preserve relevant evidence before systems rotate logs, messages are deleted,

00:00:07.488 --> 00:00:10.444
tickets are modified, or links are shared further.

00:00:10.444 --> 00:00:13.768
At the same time, avoid amplifying the questionable claim.

00:00:13.768 --> 00:00:17.832
Do not forward sensitive artifacts broadly just because they seem important.

00:00:17.832 --> 00:00:22.265
Use approved channels, need-to-know sharing, and clear handling labels when privacy, legal,

00:00:22.265 --> 00:00:24.851
human resources, or investigative concerns may apply.

00:00:24.851 --> 00:00:27.806
Verification should use trusted channels and defined ownership.

00:00:27.806 --> 00:00:31.869
If the claim involves identity, use identity and access management records.

00:00:31.869 --> 00:00:35.933
If it involves a vendor, use procurement or vendor management contacts.

00:00:35.933 --> 00:00:39.996
If it involves a security incident, use the incident command structure.

00:00:39.996 --> 00:00:44.060
If it involves potential fraud, use the fraud or risk process.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:47.385
The analyst does not need to own every decision.

00:00:47.385 --> 00:00:51.818
The analyst needs to route the question to the right owner with

00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:53.665
the right evidence and uncertainty.

00:00:53.665 --> 00:00:56.251
Communication should separate facts, assumptions, and judgments.

00:00:56.251 --> 00:00:58.836
Facts are observed records or preserved artifacts.

00:00:58.836 --> 00:01:01.792
Assumptions are working beliefs that may be tested.

00:01:01.792 --> 00:01:05.116
Judgments are analytic conclusions based on the current evidence.

00:01:05.116 --> 00:01:09.549
A useful update might say what was claimed, which artifacts were preserved,

00:01:09.549 --> 00:01:13.244
which sources were checked, what was corroborated, what remains unknown,

00:01:13.244 --> 00:01:17.307
what confidence level is appropriate, and what decision is needed next.

00:01:17.307 --> 00:01:19.154
Governance makes the practice repeatable.

00:01:19.154 --> 00:01:22.479
Playbooks should define escalation paths, evidence handling, privacy boundaries,

00:01:22.479 --> 00:01:25.434
chain of custody expectations, confidence language, approval workflows,

00:01:25.434 --> 00:01:26.542
and after-action review.

00:01:26.542 --> 00:01:30.975
Metrics should reward timely verification and good documentation, not just fast closure.

00:01:30.975 --> 00:01:35.778
After each meaningful review, improve the playbook: which signal mattered, which source helped,

00:01:35.778 --> 00:01:40.580
which step caused delay, and which control would make the next decision cleaner.
